W1       THE INSTRUMENT GAP -- and why the linear part CANNOT decide freeness
         SnapPy Isometry exposes: ['cusp_images', 'cusp_maps', 'extends_to_link', 'num_cusps']
         a translation accessor exists: False
         THE ALGEBRA, not an assertion.  An orientation-reversing involution of the
         cusp torus acts as x -> Ax + b on R^2/Z^2 with A in GL(2,Z), A^2 = I,
         det A = -1.  In the eigenbasis A = diag(1, -1), so
           (x, y) -> (x + b1, -y + b2)
         and a fixed point needs x + b1 = x (mod 1) AND -y + b2 = y (mod 1).
         The second is ALWAYS solvable (y = b2/2).  The first holds iff b1 = 0 (mod 1).
         So freeness depends ONLY on b1 -- a translation -- and NOT on A at all.
         EXHIBIT: two involutions with the SAME linear part, one free, one not.
           A = diag(1,-1), b = ('0', '0'):  fixed points found (denominator | 12): 24   -> HAS FIXED POINTS
           A = diag(1,-1), b = ('1/2', '0'):  fixed points found (denominator | 12): 0   -> FREE
         same linear part, opposite freeness: True
W1 PASS  L194's instrument gap is REAL and is now PROVED, not
